{
  "configuration_id": "config_007",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_004 (multimodal, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, concat) achieves 0.6186 acc, while config_029 (RNA-only, same pca_n2000) achieves 0.5907 and config_039 (image-only, resnet50) only 0.3275. RNA is clearly the dominant signal, and the multimodal gain over RNA-alone is modest (+2.8%). This suggests the RNA representation may be the bottleneck. Question: Does replacing the simple PCA encoder (pca_n2000) with a learned variational autoencoder (scvi_n200) improve performance in the multimodal setting? config_007 changes only the RNA encoder relative to config_004, keeping image encoder (resnet50), aggregation (avgpool), fusion (concat), and profile setting (multiprofile) identical. This clean single-variable comparison isolates the effect of RNA representation quality. Why best next experiment: Since RNA drives most of the signal, improving its encoder is the highest-leverage design decision. A positive result would justify further investment in more complex RNA encoders (e.g., scgpt); a null result would indicate PCA is sufficient and redirect attention to fusion or image-encoder choices. Under a limited budget, this experiment maximally reduces uncertainty about the most impactful design axis."
}

